', "[AI]:
The matrix for reflecting through a plane $P$ passing through the origin is given by
\[\mathbf{R} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{11}{15} & \frac{2}{15} & \frac{2}{3} \\ \frac{2}{15} & \frac{14}{15} & -\frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} & -\frac{2}{3} \end{pmatrix}.\]
A reflection through a plane with normal vector $\mathbf{n}$ satisfies the property that $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{n} = -\mathbf{n}$. This is equivalent to saying that $\mathbf{n}$ is in the null space of the matrix $\mathbf{M} = \mathbf{R} + \mathbf{I}$, where $\mathbf{I}$ is the identity matrix.

We compute $\mathbf{M} = \mathbf{R} + \mathbf{I}$:
\[\mathbf{M} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{11}{15} + 1 & \frac{2}{15} & \frac{2}{3} \\ \frac{2}{15} & \frac{14}{15} + 1 & -\frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} & -\frac{2}{3} + 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{26}{15} & \frac{2}{15} & \frac{2}{3} \\ \frac{2}{15} & \frac{29}{15} & -\frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} & \frac{1}{3} \end{pmatrix}.\]
To find the null space, we solve $\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n} = \mathbf{0}$. We can find a vector in the null space by taking the cross product of any two non-parallel rows of $\mathbf{M}$. Let's use the first and third rows:
$\mathbf{r}_1 = \left( \frac{26}{15}, \frac{2}{15}, \frac{2}{3} \right)$ and $\mathbf{r}_3 = \left( \frac{2}{3}, -\frac{1}{3}, \frac{1}{3} \right)$.
The cross product $\mathbf{r}_1 \times \mathbf{r}_3$ is:
\[\mathbf{n}' = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ \frac{26}{15} & \frac{2}{15} & \frac{2}{3} \\ \frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} & \frac{1}{3} \end{vmatrix} = \mathbf{i} \left( \frac{2}{15} \cdot \frac{1}{3} - \frac{2}{3} \cdot \left(-\frac{1}{3}\right) \right) - \mathbf{j} \left( \frac{26}{15} \cdot \frac{1}{3} - \frac{2}{3} \cdot \frac{2}{3} \right) + \mathbf{k} \left( \frac{26}{15} \cdot \left(-\frac{1}{3}\right) - \frac{2}{15} \cdot \frac{2}{3} \right)\]
\[= \mathbf{i} \left( \frac{2}{45} + \frac{2}{9} \right) - \mathbf{j} \left( \frac{26}{45} - \frac{4}{9} \right) + \mathbf{k} \left( -\frac{26}{45} - \frac{4}{45} \right)\]
\[= \mathbf{i} \left( \frac{2+10}{45} \right) - \mathbf{j} \left( \frac{26-20}{45} \right) + \mathbf{k} \left( -\frac{30}{45} \right) = \left( \frac{12}{45}, -\frac{6}{45}, -\frac{30}{45} \right).\]
Multiplying by $\frac{45}{6}$ to simplify, we get:
\[\mathbf{n} = \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}.\]
We check the conditions: $a = 2 > 0$, and $\gcd(|2|, |-1|, |-5|) = \gcd(2, 1, 5) = 1$.
Finally, we verify $\mathbf{R}\mathbf{n} = -\mathbf{n}$:
\[\begin{pmatrix} \frac{11}{15} & \frac{2}{15} & \frac{2}{3} \\ \frac{2}{15} & \frac{14}{15} & -\frac{1}{3} \\ \frac{2}{3} & -\frac{1}{3} & -\frac{2}{3} \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{22}{15} - \frac{2}{15} - \frac{10}{3} \\ \frac{4}{15} - \frac{14}{15} + \frac{5}{3} \\ \frac{4}{3} + \frac{1}{3} + \frac{10}{3} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{20}{15} - \frac{50}{15} \\ -\frac{10}{15} + \frac{25}{15} \\ \frac{15}{3} \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} -2 \\ 1 \\ 5 \end{pmatrix} = -\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}.\]
The condition is satisfied.

\boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ -1 \\ -5 \end{pmatrix}}
